; =============================================================================
; Memory to Register Promotion (mem2reg) for E-Graph Optimization
; =============================================================================
;
; mem2reg promotes memory operations to SSA values when safe. This is one of
; the most important optimizations for SPIR-V because GPU shader compilers
; start with all values in memory (OpVariable) and need to promote them to
; registers for efficient execution.
;
; Key insight for e-graphs: We don't actually "delete" memory ops. Instead,
; we establish equivalences so that loads return known values and stores
; become dead (eliminated by DCE).
;
; Promotion criteria:
; - Variable is only accessed via Load/Store (no address escapes)
; - All accesses go through the same pointer (no aliasing)
; - Value is dominated by its definition
;
; In e-graph terms:
; - Load(var, StoreMem(var, val, _)) = val (the stored value)
; - StoreMem(var, val, prev) where val is never loaded = prev (dead store)
;
; =============================================================================
; SECTION 1: Direct Load-After-Store (Core mem2reg)
; =============================================================================
; The fundamental promotion rule: loading immediately after storing gives
; back the stored value.

; Load from ptr after storing val to ptr = val (already in memory.egg)
; (rule ((= e (Load ptr (StoreMem ptr val prev))))
;       ((union e val)))

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 2: SSA Value Construction Through Memory
; =============================================================================
; When a variable is stored in multiple places and loaded after a merge,
; we construct the equivalent SSA phi/gamma.

; Load from merged memory state = Gamma of values from both branches
; If we stored v1 in true branch and v2 in false branch to same ptr,
; loading after merge gives Gamma(cond, v1, v2)
(rule ((= e (Load ptr (MergeMem cond (StoreMem ptr v1 s1) (StoreMem ptr v2 s2)))))
      ((union e (Gamma cond v1 v2))))

; Special case: only one branch stores, other passes through
; Load from merge where true branch stores, false branch doesn't modify
(rule ((= e (Load ptr (MergeMem cond (StoreMem ptr val _) prev))))
      ((union e (Gamma cond val (Load ptr prev)))))

(rule ((= e (Load ptr (MergeMem cond prev (StoreMem ptr val _)))))
      ((union e (Gamma cond (Load ptr prev) val))))

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 3: Loop Variable Promotion
; =============================================================================
; Variables updated in a loop can become Theta nodes (loop-carried values).

; Load from loop memory state = loop variable (initial value + update)
; LoopMem represents the memory state after each loop iteration
(rule ((= e (Load ptr (LoopMem cond (StoreMem ptr val body_mem) init_mem))))
      ((union e (Theta cond val (Load ptr init_mem)))))

; If the stored value doesn't depend on the loop, hoist it
(rule ((= e (Load ptr (LoopMem cond (StoreMem ptr (LoopInvariant val) body_mem) init_mem))))
      ((union e (LoopInvariant val))))

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 4: Undef Propagation
; =============================================================================
; Loading from uninitialized memory gives Undef

; Store of Undef followed by load = Undef
(rule ((= e (Load ptr (StoreMem ptr (Undef) prev))))
      ((union e (Undef))))

; Load from initial memory state (before any store) could be undef
; But we can't assume this without knowing the variable's initialization

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 5: Address Computation Optimization
; =============================================================================
; Promote variables even when accessed through constant access chains

; Load through access chain after store through same access chain
; (Already in memory.egg, but we add more patterns)

; Nested access chain promotion
; Load(AccessChain1(var, 0), StoreMem(AccessChain1(var, 0), val, _)) = val
; (Already handled by basic load-after-store with access chain pointer)

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 6: Variable Splitting
; =============================================================================
; A struct variable can be split into multiple scalar variables if each
; field is accessed independently.

; Load of composite followed by extract = Load of field directly
; (Already in memory.egg: CompositeExtract(Load(...)) = Load(AccessChain(...)))

; Store of constructed composite = sequence of field stores
; This enables individual field promotion
; (See sroa.egg for detailed composite decomposition)

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 7: Promotable Variable Detection
; =============================================================================
; Mark variables as promotable when they meet criteria.
; Promotable node is an annotation that helps extraction choose the SSA form.

; A Variable that's only loaded/stored (no address taken) is promotable
; We model this by allowing the e-graph to represent both forms:
; - Memory form: Variable + Load/Store
; - SSA form: Direct values with Gamma/Theta for control flow

; When both forms exist, extraction prefers SSA (no memory operations)

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 8: Private/Function Variable Promotion
; =============================================================================
; Variables in Private or Function storage class are best candidates
; for promotion because they can't alias with other pointers.

; Function-local variable with simple access pattern: fully promotable
; The variable's value IS the SSA value, no memory needed

; When a function-local variable is stored once and read multiple times,
; all loads return that one stored value
(rule ((= e1 (Load var mem))
       (= e2 (Load var mem)))
      ((union e1 e2)))

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 9: Partial Promotion
; =============================================================================
; Even when full promotion isn't possible, we can partially promote.

; If a variable is mostly used as SSA but occasionally its address is taken,
; we can still promote the SSA-friendly uses and keep memory for the rest.

; For now, we rely on SROA and field-level promotion for partial cases.

; =============================================================================
; SECTION 10: Consistency with Other Passes
; =============================================================================
; mem2reg works together with:
; - SROA: Splits aggregates, enabling per-field promotion
; - DCE: Eliminates the now-dead stores after promotion
; - Copy propagation: Propagates promoted values
; - CSE: Combines redundant promoted computations

; These all happen in one e-graph pass via equality saturation!
